The '''2021 Baliscan confederal election''' was held on 14 October 2021, thirty days after the dissolution of the [[Confederal Congress]]. Voters elected all 736 members of the Congress. The election was called following [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|significant public dissatisfaction]] with political deadlock in government. The election took place five days after the [[2021 Castejón regional election]], which was considered a major {{W|bellwether}} of public attitude regarding the typical moderate left-right political establishment.

The election saw a record number of {{W|Early voting|ballots cast early}} and {{W|Postal voting|by mail}} due to the ongoing [[W:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]]. This heavily benefited left-leaning candidates, whose supporters are typically less-motivated and more likely to stay home on election day. The election resulted in the returning of a [[United Left]] {{W|supermajority}}, controlling of 80% all seats in Congress. 

In the summer of 2021 the majority of the Baliscan electorate (including the overwhelming majority of self-identified [[Federalism in Balisca|federalists]]) supported the next confederal elections being moved forward. In the elections the [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] lost all of it's seats, and a significant number of the delegates previously aligned with the Bloc were defeated as the electorate rallied around anti-capitalist, anti-statist and [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] candidates.

